  "shared": {
    "translations": {
      "en": {
        "title": "The input desk",
        "story": "A welcome desk receives a name on a paper slip. This program reads one input line and prints a greeting containing that name. The supplied input is Rin followed by a newline, which marks the end of the line. Predict the complete greeting before running. Unlike the earlier signs, the name comes from the input area rather than being written inside the source message. Changing the input can therefore change the output while the program itself stays the same. An empty line is still a line.",
        "idea": "The provided reading statement stores the input text under the name name. Think of that identifier as a label for the text just read; the next module explains names and types in detail. The + operator joins the fixed string Welcome, with that stored text. Its fixed string includes a space after the comma, so the name does not touch the punctuation. The newline ending the input is not part of the stored name. Spaces inside the name are part of its text and should stay intact. Read this setup as supplied plumbing, not syntax you must memorize yet.",
        "make": "Change the fixed greeting to Hi, while keeping the input reading setup and the name unchanged. The expected result for Rin is Hi, Rin. Predict what would happen with a name containing a space, then try the input exercises below. Inspect the worked example before completing a missing greeting. The independent exercise checks several names, including an empty line, so copying the example name into the output is not a general solution. Preserve the line you received instead.",
        "recap": "Input supplies text to a running program. Source code describes how to use that text, and output shows the result. In this example, the identifier name holds one line and the output statement combines it with a fixed greeting. A correct solution works for the stated input rules, including names with spaces. Explain which part stays fixed and which part comes from the slip. Then close the code and retrieve those roles from memory. You can revisit the provided input setup whenever you need it; the next module develops the naming idea more carefully."
      },
      "fil": null,
      "ceb": null
    },
    "translationStatus": {
      "en": "reviewed",
      "fil": "draft",
      "ceb": "draft"
    }
  },
